Change only the members named. An empty value clears one, as in --model "". The result is checked as a whole profile, so one update reaches any valid profile.

--key reads a new key as create does, into the OS keychain, or into profiles.json with --file. --provider keeps base_url, account_id and model, and names on stderr the ones it keeps. On a profile that holds a key it needs --key, since a key belongs to one provider. An account_id kept on a provider that takes none is refused, so clear it with --account-id "" in the same update.

--name renames the profile and moves its keychain item, and the default follows it. An empty key typed at the prompt drops the stored key and its keychain item, when the base URL is not the provider's own. An empty key on stdin never drops a key.

Usage:
  onesie profile update NAME [flags]

Examples:
  onesie profile update work --model typesafe/jev-1.13
  pass show work | head -n 1 | onesie profile update work --key
  onesie profile update work --name personal

Flags:
      --account-id string   cloudflare only, the account id, 32 hexadecimal characters
      --allow-http          store a plain http --base-url to a host other than this machine. Every run on the profile then needs --allow-http too
      --base-url string     the api root the profile's runs use
      --file                with --key, store the new key in profiles.json, not the OS keychain
  -h, --help                help for update
      --key                 read a new key from a prompt or stdin
      --model string        the model a run uses when it names none
      --name string         a new name for the profile. Its keychain item moves with it, and the default follows it
      --provider string     a new provider for the profile. On a profile that holds a key it needs --key

Global Flags:
      --profile string   the profile a run uses, from profiles.json in the config dir. Overrides ONESIE_PROFILE and ONESIE_PROVIDER, and otherwise the default profile runs, which onesie profile use sets
